Yes — **conceptually you are reaching for duck typing**, but if HQL is meant to be rigorous and statically checkable, I would describe the mechanism more precisely as **open structural typing with progressive refinement**.

The published HMD model lines up nicely with this. An HMD namespace is a rooted tree of `.hmd` cards, and frontmatter is optional YAML whose top level is a mapping. The specification reserves a small number of keys and explicitly says every other key is user-owned and must still be exposed to consumers. ([HyperMarkDown][1]) The TypeScript document model already reflects essentially this separation: a document has `frontmatter: object` alongside headings, anchors and blocks. ([HyperMarkDown][2])

So at the HQL level I think the right conceptual model is:

```hql
cards : List<HmdCard>
```

or perhaps simply:

```hql
cards : List<Card>
```

where `Card` is the HQL-facing name and `HmdCard` is the implementation/domain name.

A card is not just the document body. It is something closer to:

```text
Card
    document
    metadata
```

and frontmatter is one source of metadata, not the definition of metadata itself.

That distinction becomes important once HQL extensions exist.

At the lowest level, metadata is simply:

```hql
card.metadata : Data
```

The YAML frontmatter contributes a tree into that `Data`. HMD itself already knows more than that about a few paths. For example, HMD knows that `tags` is a list of strings and that `nav` has a particular structure; arbitrary other keys remain uninterpreted by HMD. ([HyperMarkDown][1])

So the type can begin broad:

```text
Data
```

and HMD can refine part of it conceptually into something like:

```text
Data
& {
    tags?: List<String>
    use?: ...
    import?: ...
    nav?: ...
}
```

I would **not** necessarily make that literal HQL syntax. It is just a useful way of describing what the type checker knows.

Then suppose you load an extension:

```hql
import knowledge
```

and that extension declares that metadata under `knowledge` has a known structure. The same value has not been converted into another object. The extension has added knowledge about the shape of an existing `Data` tree.

Before loading the extension:

```hql
card.metadata.knowledge : Data?
```

After the extension contributes its schema:

```hql
card.metadata.knowledge : KnowledgeMetadata?
```

And deeper paths become known too:

```hql
card.metadata.knowledge.concepts : List<Concept>
```

This is the interesting part: **the runtime value has not changed; the compiler's knowledge of the value has changed.**

You could think of the process as:

```text
YAML
  ↓
Data
  ↓ HMD knows some paths
Data refined by HMD
  ↓ extensions contribute schemas
Data refined by HMD + extension schemas
  ↓ program tests existence / shape
locally refined still further
```

That last stage is where it starts feeling very much like duck typing.

Suppose arbitrary author metadata contains:

```yaml
database:
  engine: postgres
  host: db.example.org
```

Initially:

```hql
card.metadata.database : Data?
```

But perhaps PostgreSQL support defines a structural expectation corresponding roughly to:

```text
{
    engine: "postgres"
    host: String
}
```

If that shape is established, HQL can safely treat that subtree as a `PostgresConfig`.

The crucial property is that **the object does not have to declare itself to be a `PostgresConfig`**.

It becomes usable as one because it has the required structure.

That is exactly the intuition behind duck typing:

> If it has the required shape, treat it accordingly.

But traditional duck typing usually means the language simply attempts the operation at runtime:

```python
x.quack()
```

and if `x` has `quack`, fine.

What you are describing for HQL is stronger. HQL would establish the shape and then type-check operations against that established shape. So:

```text
duck typing
    "it looks like a Duck, use it as one"

HQL
    "we can prove from the known structure that this value satisfies Duck"
```

That is much closer to **structural typing**.

And because the knowledge can become stronger as HQL processes HMD semantics, imported extensions, predicates, schemas, and perhaps constraints, it is specifically a form of **progressive type refinement**.

This fits extremely well with your longer-term Lean-like direction too. You can imagine:

```hql
x : Data
```

then some operation establishes:

```text
has(x, "host")
typeof(x.host) == String
```

after which:

```hql
x.host
```

is no longer an unsafe dynamic access. The type checker has evidence for it.

So I would avoid calling `Data` itself a dynamic type. It isn't really `Any`.

`Data` says something quite strong already:

```text
This is a valid structured tree.
```

Then schemas and extensions say increasingly stronger things about particular regions of that tree.

For a card, the model starts looking beautifully simple:

```text
Card
├── document
└── metadata : Data
```

HMD contributes meaning to part of `metadata`.

Extensions contribute more meaning.

User code can narrow it further.

And the whole thing remains one underlying tree rather than repeatedly converting between `Data`, `Frontmatter`, `KnowledgeMetadata`, `PostgresMetadata`, etc.

So I think **"rigorous duck typing" is actually a very good informal description**.

The technical description I would put into the language design, though, is something like:

> **HQL uses open structural data with progressive type refinement. `Data` values retain their original tree representation while HMD semantics, extensions, schemas, and predicates progressively establish stronger types for parts of that tree.**

And there is one important consequence: an extension should probably **not own metadata**. It owns **type knowledge about metadata**.

That is a much more powerful architecture.

[1]: https://hypermarkdown.org/wiki/hmd-lang-spec/ "Language Specification - HyperMarkDown"
[2]: https://hypermarkdown.org/proposals/HMD-0020/ "HMD-0020: @hypermarkdown/core — the TypeScript document model - HyperMarkDown"
